
                        EIN. MEER. VON. SAND.
                a demo for MS-DOS  ú  Kasper Bergholt  ú  2026

  °±²Û THE WORK Û²±°

  In 1738 a monument was raised at Tisvilde, on the north coast of Zealand,
  where the drifting sand had finally been stopped. Its faces speak Danish,
  German and Latin; the Latin face is overgrown with moss.

  This demo reads the Danish and German faces, and an English translation,
  side by side. One rule turns every letter into sound: the letter becomes a
  partial of a fixed A2 (110 Hz), given by its place in the alphabet modulo M.
  Danish AE, OE and AA continue the alphabet as 27, 28 and 29. The modulus
  drifts from 12 to 17 over the piece, so the same letter changes its tone.

  Every letter that has been said leaves the text and settles in the sea at
  the bottom of the screen. The waves grow as the sediment grows, and the drone
  keeps every letter at the tuning it had when it was said. Vowels swell and
  hold; consonants strike. Words the languages share (sand, Gram, Rohl,
  Frederik, Christian, sea, wind, 1738) light up in all three columns at once.
  Twice, everything stops.

  °±²Û RUNNING IT Û²±°

  Needs a 386 or better, VGA and a Sound Blaster 16 (A220 I7 D1).
  In DOSBox: double-click RUN.BAT, or

       mount c c:\sand
       c:
       sand            (ESC quits; SAND 300 starts five minutes in)

  The picture follows the sound card, so the timing stays exact even when
  the frame rate drops as the sea fills up.

